// Pathological witness: the certified kernel's admission boundary is narrower
// than the checked language's, and this pins both sides of it.
//
// The session offers every typed definition to the kernel through the
// elaborator-side bridge, and the kernel re-derives each one at its own choke
// point before admitting it. What crosses is the closed S1 vocabulary: the base
// atoms `Integer` / `String` / `Numeric`, products, sums, unit, thunks, and
// returners. What does not cross has not been rejected — S1 is a standing
// subset that grows, so a form outside it is a form the certified vocabulary
// has not reached yet, and the definition types, binds, and evaluates exactly
// as it would if the kernel were not there at all.
//
// `pair` is the case worth having a witness for: its body names `greeting`, so
// it can only cross by resolving that name to the admission index `greeting`
// entered at. A single-definition program never exercises that path.
//
// `wide` is the other side. A machine-numeric atom is outside the S1 base
// stock, so it stays a perfectly ordinary bound definition that the kernel does
// not carry — asserted rather than described, because a boundary nobody checks
// is a boundary that moves silently.

//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-kernel-admitted: greeting
//@ expect-kernel-admitted: pair
//@ expect-kernel-admitted: identity
//@ expect-kernel-outside-s1: wide
//@ expect-def: wide
//@ expect-last-value: "the value zone"

def greeting = "the value zone";

def pair = (greeting, greeting);

def identity(x: Integer) -> F Integer {
  ret x
}

def wide = 1u32;

ret greeting
